\keywords{Matching, Market Design, Affirmative Action, Reserve System, Strategy-Proofness, Serial Dictatorship.}

\abstract{Reserve systems are a standard tool for affirmative action in admissions: some seats are set aside for reserved categories, and the remaining seats are open to all students. We study a single-college model in which students are ranked by exam scores and matchings must be score-feasible: a student can be admitted through a reserved category only if her score is at most a category-specific margin below that of the highest-scoring student rejected from the open category. This requirement is motivated by affirmative action in India, where it is recommended that the gap between the cutoff scores of the open category and a reserved category be bounded. When quotas are fixed, a score-based serial dictatorship mechanism (SSD) is fair, Pareto efficient, and strategy-proof. When unfilled reserved seats can be transferred to the open category, we introduce reserved category protection and show that, together with feasibility, score-feasibility, and fairness, strategy-proofness, reserved category protection, and weak non-wastefulness cannot be satisfied simultaneously. In contrast, each pair of these three properties is achieved by a variant of SSD. Thus, we obtain a tight boundary between possibility and impossibility.}

