All entries follow from Theorems~\ref{thm:imp1}--\ref{thm:fqssd} and
Example~\ref{ex:fqssd}.
Combining them with Theorem~\ref{thm:imp2}, we obtain the following.

\begin{corollary}
\label{cor:boundary}
Assume that feasibility, score-feasibility, and fairness are required.
Then no mechanism satisfies all of SP, RCP, and WNW in general.
In contrast, for each pair of these three properties, there is a mechanism that satisfies
both properties of the pair:
\begin{itemize}
\item RCP-SSD satisfies RCP and WNW (Theorem~\ref{thm:rcpssd}),
\item flexible SSD satisfies SP and WNW; in fact, it is non-wasteful
(Theorem~\ref{thm:flexible}), and
\item fixed quota SSD satisfies SP and RCP (Theorem~\ref{thm:fqssd}).
\end{itemize}
\end{corollary}
\begin{jproof}
三つ全ての両立不可能性は Theorem~\ref{thm:imp2} である．
RCP-SSD は Theorem~\ref{thm:rcpssd} より feasibility，score-feasibility，fairness，RCP，WNW を満たす．
Flexible SSD は Theorem~\ref{thm:flexible} より feasibility，score-feasibility，fairness，SP，non-wastefulness を満たし，non-wastefulness は WNW を導く．
Fixed quota SSD は Theorem~\ref{thm:fqssd} より feasibility，score-feasibility，fairness，RCP，SP を満たす．
\end{jproof}

Thus, Corollary~\ref{cor:boundary} gives a tight boundary between possibility and
impossibility: the impossibility of Theorem~\ref{thm:imp2} disappears as soon as any one
of SP, RCP, and WNW is dropped.

The three mechanisms can also be understood in a unified way in terms of the protection
level of each reserved category $t$, i.e., the number of seats of $t$ that cannot be used
for the open category unless they are filled by students of $t$.
Flexible SSD uses $0$, RCP-SSD uses $d_t$, and fixed quota SSD uses $q_t$, where
$0\le d_t\le q_t$.
A protection level of $0$ violates RCP, and a protection level of $q_t$ is too
conservative to satisfy WNW.
The intermediate level $d_t$ satisfies both, but it depends on students' reports,
and this dependence is the source of manipulation.
